  {
    "pkattachmentId": 64060,
    "bookid": 64,
    "chapterId": 405,
    "fileId": 38180,
    "type": "Table",
    "diagnosis": "",
    "title": "<p>Diagnostic criteria for systemic mastocytosis (SM) {34901755}</p>",
    "legend": "",
    "source": "",
    "copyright": "Verstovsek S",
    "tablehtml": "<table class=\"MsoNormalTable\" style=\"width: 23.0cm; background: #DAEEF3; border-collapse: collapse; mso-yfti-tbllook: 1184; mso-padding-alt: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" border=\"0\" width=\"869\" cellspacing=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\">\r\n<tbody>\r\n<tr style=\"mso-yfti-irow: 0; mso-yfti-firstrow: yes; page-break-inside: avoid; height: 54.25pt;\">\r\n<td style=\"width: 23.0cm; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt; height: 54.25pt;\" width=\"869\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><strong><span style=\"color: black; mso-color-alt: windowtext;\">Major criterion:</span></strong></p>\r\n<p class=\"TableBullet\" style=\"mso-list: l0 level1 lfo1; tab-stops: 36.0pt;\"><span style=\"font-family: Symbol; mso-fareast-font-family: Symbol; mso-bidi-font-family: Symbol; mso-bidi-font-weight: bold;\"><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">&middot;<span style=\"font: 7.0pt 'Times New Roman';\">&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; </span></span></span><span style=\"color: black; mso-color-alt: windowtext;\">Multifocal dense infiltrates of mast cells (&ge;&nbsp;15&nbsp;mast cells in aggregates) detected in sections of bone marrow and/or other extracutaneous organ(s)</span></p>\r\n</td>\r\n</tr>\r\n<tr style=\"mso-yfti-irow: 1; page-break-inside: avoid; height: 39.05pt;\">\r\n<td style=\"width: 23.0cm; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt; height: 39.05pt;\" width=\"869\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><strong><span style=\"color: black; mso-color-alt: windowtext;\">Minor criteria:</span></strong></p>\r\n<p class=\"TableBullet\" style=\"mso-list: l0 level1 lfo1; tab-stops: 36.0pt;\"><span style=\"font-family: Symbol; mso-fareast-font-family: Symbol; mso-bidi-font-family: Symbol;\"><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">&middot;<span style=\"font: 7.0pt 'Times New Roman';\">&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; </span></span></span><span style=\"color: black; mso-color-alt: windowtext;\">&gt;&nbsp;25% of all mast cells are atypical cells (type&nbsp;I or type&nbsp;II) on bone marrow smears or are spindle-shaped in dense and diffuse mast cell infiltrates in sections of bone marrow or other extracutaneous organ(s)<sup>a</sup></span></p>\r\n<p class=\"TableBullet\" style=\"mso-list: l0 level1 lfo1; tab-stops: 36.0pt;\"><span style=\"font-family: Symbol; mso-fareast-font-family: Symbol; mso-bidi-font-family: Symbol;\"><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">&middot;<span style=\"font: 7.0pt 'Times New Roman';\">&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; </span></span></span><span style=\"color: black; mso-color-alt: windowtext;\">Activating <em>KIT</em> point mutation(s) at codon&nbsp;816 or in other critical regions of <em><span style=\"mso-no-proof: yes;\">KIT</span></em><sup><span style=\"mso-no-proof: yes;\">b</span></sup> in bone marrow or other extracutaneous organ(s)</span></p>\r\n<p class=\"TableBullet\" style=\"mso-list: l0 level1 lfo1; tab-stops: 36.0pt;\"><span style=\"font-family: Symbol; mso-fareast-font-family: Symbol; mso-bidi-font-family: Symbol;\"><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">&middot;<span style=\"font: 7.0pt 'Times New Roman';\">&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; </span></span></span><span style=\"color: black; mso-color-alt: windowtext;\">Mast cells in bone marrow, blood, or other extracutaneous organ(s) aberrantly express one or more of the following antigens: CD2, CD25, CD30<sup>c</sup></span></p>\r\n<p class=\"TableBullet\" style=\"mso-list: l0 level1 lfo1; tab-stops: 36.0pt;\"><span style=\"font-family: Symbol; mso-fareast-font-family: Symbol; mso-bidi-font-family: Symbol; mso-bidi-font-weight: bold;\"><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">&middot;<span style=\"font: 7.0pt 'Times New Roman';\">&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; </span></span></span><span style=\"color: black; mso-color-alt: windowtext;\">Baseline serum tryptase concentration &gt;&nbsp;20&nbsp;ng/mL in the absence of a myeloid <span style=\"mso-no-proof: yes;\">AHN;<sup>d</sup></span> in the case of a known H&alpha;T, the tryptase level could be <span style=\"mso-no-proof: yes;\">adjusted<sup>e</sup></span></span></p>\r\n</td>\r\n</tr>\r\n<tr style=\"mso-yfti-irow: 2; mso-yfti-lastrow: yes; page-break-inside: avoid;\">\r\n<td style=\"width: 23.0cm; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"869\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><span style=\"color: black; mso-color-alt: windowtext;\">The diagnosis is SM if the major criterion and at least one minor criterion are fulfilled, or if at least three minor criteria are fulfilled.</span></p>\r\n</td>\r\n</tr>\r\n</tbody>\r\n</table>\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><span style=\"mso-fareast-font-family: Aptos;\">AHN, associated haematological neoplasm; H&alpha;T, hereditary alpha-tryptasaemia.</span></p>\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><sup><span style=\"mso-fareast-font-family: Aptos; mso-no-proof: yes;\">a</span></sup><span style=\"mso-fareast-font-family: Aptos; mso-no-proof: yes;\">In</span><span style=\"mso-fareast-font-family: Aptos;\"> tissue sections, an abnormal mast cell morphology counts in both a dense infiltrate and a diffuse mast cell infiltrate. In the bone marrow smear, an atypical morphology of mast cells does not count as an SM criterion when the mast cells are located in or adjacent to bone marrow particles. Morphological criteria of atypical mast cells have been described </span><span class=\"Ref-PMID\">{11377686}</span><span style=\"mso-fareast-font-family: Aptos;\">. <sup><span style=\"mso-no-proof: yes;\">b</span></sup><span style=\"mso-no-proof: yes;\">Any</span> type of <em>KIT</em> mutation counts as a minor SM criterion when published solid evidence for its transforming behaviour is available (an overview of potentially activating <em>KIT</em> mutations is provided in the supplementary material of </span><span class=\"Ref-PMID\">{34901755}</span><span style=\"mso-fareast-font-family: Aptos;\">). <sup><span style=\"mso-no-proof: yes;\">c</span></sup><span style=\"mso-no-proof: yes;\">Expression</span> must be confirmed by either flow cytometry or immunohistochemistry. <sup><span style=\"mso-no-proof: yes;\">d</span></sup><span style=\"mso-no-proof: yes;\">Myeloid</span> neoplasms can lead to increased serum tryptase levels. Therefore, this criterion does not count in cases of SM with an AHN. <sup><span style=\"mso-no-proof: yes;\">e</span></sup><span style=\"mso-no-proof: yes;\">A</span> possible mode for adjustment has been proposed in </span><span class=\"Ref-PMID\">{34901755}</span><span style=\"mso-fareast-font-family: Aptos;\">. The basal tryptase level may be divided by 1&nbsp;plus the number of extra copies of the &alpha;-tryptase gene (<em>TPSAB1</em>). For example, if the tryptase level is 30&nbsp;ng/mL and two extra copies of the &alpha;-tryptase gene are found in a patient with H&alpha;T, the H&alpha;T-corrected tryptase level is 10&nbsp;ng/mL (30/3&nbsp;=&nbsp;10), thereby not meeting the level of a minor SM criterion.</span></p>",
    "imageUrl": null,
    "thumbnail": null
  },
